= PolyFEM (Dev) 2.0 =

#type: node
#context: obj
#internal: stevenabramowitch::dev::PolyFEM::2.0
#tags: fem, simulation, soft body, contact, polyfem

"""Sets up a PolyFEM simulation of deformable solids -- meshes, materials, boundary conditions, contact and solver -- runs it, and opens the results."""

== Overview ==

This node writes a PolyFEM input (`params.json` and the mesh, selection and
material files it refers to) into a __Working Directory__, runs the PolyFEM
solver on it, and follows the run. The results are read back with the
__Read PVD 1.0__ node (__Open Results__ creates one).

Every parameter has a tooltip: hover over its label for what it does, its
units and typical values.

== Quick start ==

# __Main tab__: set __PolyFEM Binary__ to your `PolyFEM_bin` and
  __Working Directory__ to a folder for this simulation. Type it as
  `$HIP/sim/` to keep it next to the scene: moving the scene folder then
  keeps every input.
# __Geometry tab__: pick a Gmsh `.msh` volume mesh (tetrahedra or
  hexahedra) in __Mesh File__. Each physical group of the file becomes a
  __subdomain__, numbered by sorted tag (elements without a group come
  last); each subdomain's header shows the group's tag, name and element
  count.
# __Material__: choose a model per subdomain -- __Neo-Hookean
  (recommended)__ suits rubber-like and soft biological materials -- and
  set Young's modulus, Poisson's ratio and density.
# __Sidesets__: add a sideset to a subdomain, pick its faces in the
  viewport (or type `*` for every face, or a selection such as
  `axis:-z:0.01`), and give it a boundary condition. A __Dirichlet__
  `[0, 0, 0]` fixes the faces; `[0, 0, "-0.1*t"]` pushes them down by 0.1
  over one unit of time. A sideset can carry several conditions, and faces
  in several sidesets receive all of their conditions.
# __Time tab__: __Quasistatic__ for slow loading (no inertia), otherwise a
  dynamic run; set the end time and the time step.
# __Main tab__: press __Check Setup__. It lists, at once, everything PolyFEM
  could not run (errors) and setups that are legal but usually wrong
  (warnings). Then press __Run in Background__ (or __Run in Terminal__ to
  watch the solver's output). __Run Status__ shows the time step and, at
  the end, what the exit status means; __Stop__ ends a run.
# Press __Open Results__: a Read PVD node loads the run, sets the playbar to
  its time steps and frames the view.

TIP:
    Picking a geometry's mesh file again reloads it and keeps every
    subdomain's settings; __Duplicate Geometry__ adds a copy with every
    setting.

== Gravity and other body loads ==

__Body Load (rhs = minus acceleration)__ on the Global tab is PolyFEM's
right-hand side, which is the __negative__ of the body acceleration. Gravity
pulling along -y is therefore
`[0, 9.81, 0]` (in m/s^2), __not__ `[0, -9.81, 0]`.

== Units ==

PolyFEM uses the units of your mesh; keep every value in one consistent
system:

table>>
    tr>>
        th>> System
        th>> Moduli, pressure
        th>> Density
        th>> Gravity
    tr>>
        td>> SI: m, kg, s
        td>> Pa
        td>> kg/m^3 (water 1000)
        td>> 9.81
    tr>>
        td>> Millimetres: mm, g, s
        td>> Pa
        td>> g/mm^3 (water 0.001)
        td>> 9810

The contact distance __dhat__ is a length in mesh units: about 1/1000 of the
model's size is a good start (Check Setup warns when it is much larger).

== When a run stops: exit statuses ==

table>>
    tr>>
        th>> Run Status
        th>> Meaning
        th>> What to do
    tr>>
        td>> Completed (exit status 0)
        td>> The run finished.
        td>> __Open Results__.
    tr>>
        td>> Failed: exit status 1
        td>> A named failure, not a crash: PolyFEM stopped and said why (the
             `PolyFEM stopped:` line, shown in Run Status).
        td>> _Reached iteration limit_: often a body nothing holds in a
             quasistatic run (fix part of it), or too large a time step or
             load increment. _element N is flipped_: inverted elements (a
             mirrored transform, a bad mesh, too large a step). A JSON error:
             a value the input cannot hold -- run Check Setup.
    tr>>
        td>> Stopped: exit status 3
        td>> A resource limit: a safety stop before memory ran out, usually
             in contact detection. The time steps already written are
             intact.
        td>> Reduce the time step or load increment, use the BVH broad phase
             (Contact > CCD Parameters), or raise the resource limits.
    tr>>
        td>> Crashed: killed by a signal
        td>> The solver crashed or was killed from outside.
        td>> Keep the log (__Open Log__) and the working directory, and
             report it.
    tr>>
        td>> Stopped by you
        td>> __Stop__ ended the run.
        td>> The steps already written can be opened.

